course: dictionary
lesson: 15413
title: Spud: a word with two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A spud is an informal word for a potato. You will hear it at Sunday dinner, when someone says, «Pass the spuds, please.» The words around it, like pass and please, and the food on the table, tell you it means potatoes.
explain: Read carefully, because spud has an old meaning too. «The gardener used a spud to pull up the weeds.» There are no dinner plates here, but there are weeds. A spud is a small digging tool with a narrow blade, so the context shows which meaning fits.
explain: Always check by swapping in your meaning. Does «Pass the digging tools, please» make sense at dinner? No, so the food meaning is right there. Using the clues around the word is how you choose the right meaning.
fact: A spud is an informal word for a potato, used in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «Spud» can also mean an old, small digging tool with a narrow blade, used for weeding.
fact: «Spuds» is the plural, so one spud, two or more spuds.
fact: «Jacket spud» is a UK phrase for a baked potato cooked in its skin.
fact: Many people in Ireland relied on potatoes for food, so when potato blight destroyed the crop in the Great Famine of 1845 to 1852, many people went hungry.
exercise: «Pass the spuds, please.» What are spuds here? | Carrots | Small digging tools | *Potatoes
exercise: «The gardener used a spud to pull up the weeds.» What is a spud in this sentence? | *A small digging tool with a narrow blade | A baked potato in its skin | A kind of bird
exercise: Which phrase means a baked potato cooked in its skin? | Hot chips | Couch potato | *Jacket spud
test: «Dad put a jacket spud in the oven for my lunch.» Is a jacket spud a potato? | No, it is a bird | No, it is a kind of boot | *Yes, it is a baked potato
test: Which is the plural of spud? | Spudses | Spud's | *Spuds
exam: «Mum baked a jacket spud for tea.» Which word tells you spud means food? | Mum | *baked | Tuesday
